  "Question": "A biased die is rolled. Can 1/6 be used as the probability of rolling a 6? Why?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Yes, because the die has 6 faces.",
    "B": "No, because the scores are not equally likely, so the equal-likelihood requirement is not satisfied.",
    "C": "Yes, because rolling a 6 is only 1 outcome.",
    "D": "No, because there are infinitely many outcomes."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The value 1/6 is calculated using \\(P=m/n\\), which requires all outcomes to be equally likely. When the die is biased, the scores are not equally likely, so 1/6 cannot be used directly.",
